# Normalize text in the repository: keep LF in git.
# Without this, a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true rewrites gradlew to
# CRLF and the launcher dies on Linux/macOS with "bad interpreter".
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows launchers must stay CRLF -- and are stored VERBATIM (not normalized
# to LF). Two reasons:
#   1. gradlew.bat contains goto/label, and cmd.exe mis-parses LF-only batch
#      files (the classic "wrong line" symptom on Windows).
#   2. Keeping them verbatim makes the release zip byte-identical to the repo
#      blobs, so the published package can be checked against the repo with a
#      plain sha1 comparison -- zero differences, no exceptions.
*.bat -text
*.cmd -text

# PowerShell scripts also stay VERBATIM (not normalized to LF), for two reasons:
#   1. They contain Chinese text, so they need a UTF-8 BOM -- otherwise
#      PowerShell 5.1 decodes them with the system ANSI code page (GBK here)
#      and mangles the text. The BOM is part of the file, so it must not be
#      rewritten by a checkout either.
#   2. Keeping them verbatim makes the release zip byte-identical to the repo
#      blobs, so the published package can be checked against the repo with a
#      plain sha1 comparison -- zero differences, no exceptions.
*.ps1 -text

# Binary payloads (not tracked today, but be explicit).
*.zip binary
*.jar binary
*.aar binary
*.png binary
*.so binary
